"A QUERY READS WHAT THE STORE HOLDS, A FULLY DERIVED HEAD INCLUDED (2026-10-03). On support, query [sel, ObjectTypeInstanceIsCurrentlyInStatus, 1, dc-2026-10-02-churn-evidence] answered nothing while get showed the instance at Applied. query took derive:store_pairs, the seed a closure starts from, where a head the rule owns has no rows of its own. A query is a reader: it reads the populations its recipe names as system:pop_rows reads them, where get reads them too. ThingHasOwner is asserted.",
"AND A FULLY DERIVED HEAD IS WHAT ITS RULE COMPUTES WHEN IT IS READ (2026-10-04, task #172). ThingIsInStatus is marked fully derived, and the store computes it on read: its rule pairs each started Thing with Applied, so t1 is Applied, and the cell of its name, which holds t1 Applied and a stale t2 Proposed no rule derives, is not what a read answers.",
DEF("fixture:query-store", K(S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S3(S5(A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Status")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("ThingIsStarted"), S1(A("Thing")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S1(S1(A("t1"))))), S5(A("ThingHasOwner"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Owner")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:derived"), S1(S1(S2(A("ThingIsInStatus"), A("full"))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), S1(S1(S3(A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Status")), S3(A("pairwith"), S3(A("proj"), A("ThingIsStarted"), S1(N(1))), A("Applied")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("Applied")), S2(A("t2"), A("Proposed")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S1(S2(A("query"), A("recipe-and-populations")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ThingHasOwner"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("sam"))))))))),
DEF("case:a-query-reads-a-fully-derived-head-as-its-rule-computes-it", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("query"), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:verb_pair"), S3(A("CONS"), A("fixture:query-store"), K(S2(A("query"), S4(A("sel"), A("ThingIsInStatus"), N(1), A("t1"))))))), PHI())),
DEF("case:a-query-reads-no-row-of-a-fully-derived-head-its-rule-does-not-compute", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("query"), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:verb_pair"), S3(A("CONS"), A("fixture:query-store"), K(S2(A("query"), S4(A("sel"), A("ThingIsInStatus"), N(1), A("t2"))))))), PHI())),
DEF("case:a-query-joins-a-fully-derived-head-with-an-asserted-one", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("query"), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:verb_pair"), S3(A("CONS"), A("fixture:query-store"), K(S2(A("query"), S5(A("joinon"), A("ThingIsInStatus"), A("ThingHasOwner"), S1(S2(N(1), N(1))), S3(N(1), N(2), N(4)))))))), PHI())),
"AND A CONSTANT IS NOT A SOURCE (2026-10-03). pairwith, calc and starts read slot 2 alone: slot 3 is the value paired, the operator, or the column a prefix is tested on. derive:reads listed them with the two-source forms, so each reported reading its constant as a fact type, as a sum once reported reading its columns.",
DEF("case:reads-pairwith-one-source", S2(A("derive:reads"), S3(A("pairwith"), A("F1"), A("open")))),
DEF("case:reads-calc-one-source", S2(A("derive:reads"), S5(A("calc"), A("F1"), A("+"), N(1), N(2)))),
DEF("case:reads-starts-one-source", S2(A("derive:reads"), S4(A("starts"), A("F1"), N(1), A("dc-")))),
"AND ask READS WHAT THE STORE HOLDS, AND derive STARTS FROM THE ASSERTED POPULATIONS (2026-10-03). ask takes arguments-and-populations and answered a fully derived head with no rows, since it was handed a closure`s seed; derive takes rules-and-asserted-populations, the seed, and must start such a head from nothing whatever its cell holds. Here ThingIsInStatus is fully derived, and its cell holds t1 Applied, which its rule derives from ThingIsStarted, and t9 Stale, which nothing derives. ask answers both rows; derive with the rule answers t1 Applied alone, as it did before.",
"AND ask READS A FULLY DERIVED HEAD AS ITS RULE COMPUTES IT (2026-10-04, task #172). The store now carries ThingIsInStatus`s rule, and a head marked `*` is computed when it is read, so ask answers t1 Applied, which the rule derives, and not the stale t9 its cell still holds.",
DEF("fixture:ask-store", K(S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S4(S5(A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Status")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("ThingIsStarted"), S1(A("Thing")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S1(S1(A("t1"))))), S5(A("ThingHasOwner"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Owner")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("t1"), A("sam"))))), S5(A("FactTypeHasDerivationMode"), S2(A("Fact Type"), A("Derivation Mode")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("ThingIsInStatus"), A("fully-derived")))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:derived"), S1(S1(S2(A("ThingIsInStatus"), A("full"))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), S1(S1(S3(A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Status")), S3(A("pairwith"), S3(A("proj"), A("ThingIsStarted"), S1(N(1))), A("Applied")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ThingIsInStatus"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("Applied")), S2(A("t9"), A("Stale")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("ask"), A("arguments-and-populations")), S2(A("derive"), A("rules-and-asserted-populations"))))))))),
DEF("case:an-ask-reads-a-fully-derived-head-as-its-rule-computes-it", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ask"), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:verb_pair"), S3(A("CONS"), A("fixture:ask-store"), K(S2(A("ask"), S1(S2(N(1), A("ThingIsInStatus")))))))), PHI())),
DEF("case:derive-starts-a-head-it-owns-from-nothing", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:pop"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("ThingIsInStatus")), S3(A("COMP"), A("derive"), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:verb_pair"), S3(A("CONS"), A("fixture:ask-store"), K(S2(A("derive"), S1(S2(A("ThingIsInStatus"), S3(A("pairwith"), S3(A("proj"), A("ThingIsStarted"), S1(N(1))), A("Applied"))))))))))), PHI())),
DEF("case:derive-is-handed-the-asserted-populations", S2(A("main:verb_shape"), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("ask"), A("arguments-and-populations")), S2(A("derive"), A("rules-and-asserted-populations"))))))), S1(A("derive"))))),
"AND THE LAST TWO UNDECLARED OPERATIONS TOOK TWO DIFFERENT THINGS (2026-09-11). validate takes the fact type DESCRIPTORS, a construction the table did not have: over the base store the store state raised `expected sequence, got atom` and the population pairs raised `selector 5 out of range 2`, so validate was reachable through the route and could not answer whatever it was handed. derive takes the population pairs it always took, and what kept it off the surface was the ARGUMENT half -- an address of one element has no second element, and `2 o 2` on it threw `selector 2 out of range 1` before derive ran at all. The third case is why the store here carries a row: a verb called with no arguments must be handed the empty argument AND the store's population, and without the length guard that call is a refusal rather than a wrong answer.",
DEF("case:verb-declared-to-take-the-descriptors", S2(A("main:verb_shape"), S2(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("x"), A("1")))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("validate"), A("descriptor-list")), S2(A("derive"), A("arguments-and-populations"))))))), S1(A("validate"))))),
DEF("case:verb-taking-descriptors-is-handed-the-fact-types", S2(A("main:verb_pair"), S2(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("x"), A("1")))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("validate"), A("descriptor-list")), S2(A("derive"), A("arguments-and-populations"))))))), S1(A("validate"))))),
DEF("case:verb-called-with-no-arguments-gets-the-empty-argument", S2(A("main:verb_pair"), S2(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("x"), A("1")))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("validate"), A("descriptor-list")), S2(A("derive"), A("arguments-and-populations"))))))), S1(A("derive"))))),
"A HEAD A RULE DELIVERS IS SERVED, AND IT IS SERVED ONCE (2026-09-11). The oracle writes no descriptor for a fully derived fact type -- Codd 1970 1.5, it is strong redundancy as a stored relation -- so state:declared omits it while its rows are computed at boot and answered by system:pop_rows. mcp:tools read state:declared and offered none of them: 53 on support.auto.dev, 11 with rows, `Predicate is bound` and `Constraint awaits a decider` among them. Here AHasB is declared AND has a rule, CIsD only has a rule: the first must appear exactly once, which is the whole job of the membership guard, and the second must appear at all. The second case is the absence: a store with no state:rules cell answers its declared schema rather than raising, which is what ast:fetch's # would do to theta:flatten.",
DEF("case:a-rule-head-is-served-though-it-has-no-descriptor", S2(A("mcp:tools"), S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), S1(S2(S3(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")), S3(A("proj"), A("XHasY"), S2(N(1), N(2)))), S3(A("CIsD"), S2(A("C"), A("D")), S3(A("proj"), A("AHasB"), S2(N(1), N(2))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:readings"), S1(S1(S3(A("CIsD"), S2(A("C"), A("D")), S1(S3(A("{0}"), A("is"), A("{1}")))))))))),
DEF("case:a-store-with-no-rules-serves-its-declared-schema", S2(A("mcp:tools"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B"))))))))),
"AND THE FIFTH CONSTRUCTION HAD NO CASE UNTIL A VERB TOOK IT. `cells` has been in main:verb_shapes since the table was written and nothing was declared to accept it, so the branch that hands a verb the whole store was the one arm of five nothing held. verify accepts it, measured 2026-09-11: verify IS law:all, it answers 'T' applied to the cells and raises `expected sequence, got atom` applied to store:state. Removing the arm would leave this call falling through to the default pair, which is a PAIR and not the cells, and every other case in this family would still pass.",
DEF("case:verb-declared-to-take-the-cells", S2(A("main:verb_pair"), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S1(S2(A("verify"), A("cells"))))))), S1(A("verify"))))),
"AND THE VERB SURFACE IS THE OPERATIONS THE MODEL DESCRIBES AND THE STORE CAN RUN (2026-09-10). Both halves matter: v1 and v2 are catalogued operations declared to accept the store, and only v1 has a cell the route could apply, so only v1 is listed. Listing v2 would advertise a verb whose call could not answer -- which is the failure mode the two hand-written verb tables died of, eight of eight answering operand errors. The yields is carried through and an operation with none says nothing rather than dropping out.",
DEF("case:verbs-are-the-runnable-declared-operations", S2(A("mcp:verbs"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("v1"), S1(A("id"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("OperationIsOverridable"), S2(S1(A("v1")), S1(A("v2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("OperationIsRegistrable"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("v1"), A("store")), S2(A("v2"), A("store")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionYieldsTypeExpression"), S1(S2(A("v1"), A("cell-list"))))))))),
"AND A VERB CARRIES ITS DESCRIPTION (2026-10-01). The fixture above with each verb given a Function has Description: the row answers it fourth, so the tool a host builds from it leads with what the verb is for, and v2, which the store cannot run, is still not listed however it is described.",
DEF("case:a-verb-carries-its-description", S2(A("mcp:verbs"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("v1"), S1(A("id"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("OperationIsOverridable"), S2(S1(A("v1")), S1(A("v2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("OperationIsRegistrable"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionAcceptsTypeExpression"), S2(S2(A("v1"), A("store")), S2(A("v2"), A("store")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionYieldsTypeExpression"), S1(S2(A("v1"), A("cell-list")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionHasDescription"), S2(S2(A("v1"), A("answers the cells")), S2(A("v2"), A("never listed"))))))))),
"A DOMAIN DESCRIPTION IS A FUNCTION DESCRIPTION WHOSE SUBJECT IS A DOMAIN (2026-10-01). FunctionHasDescription holds every Function`s Description since the subtype ones were retired, and orient and the vanity layer read Domains only, so system:domain_descriptions keeps the rows whose subject the store files under Domain, in the order they stand. d1 and d2 are Domains and query, a verb with a Description, is not.",
DEF("case:domain-descriptions-keep-the-domains", S2(A("system:domain_descriptions"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionHasDescription"), S3(S2(A("d1"), A("Top")), S2(A("d2"), A("Child")), S2(A("query"), A("A verb")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("d1"), A("Domain")), S2(A("d2"), A("Domain"))))))),
"AND THE VANITY LAYER SPEAKS THE LAST DOMAIN, NOT THE LAST DESCRIPTION. It read the last DomainHasDescription row; over the one population the last row here is the verb`s, and the layer still names d2 with its Description.",
DEF("case:vanity-speaks-the-last-domain", S2(A("ui:vanity"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionHasDescription"), S3(S2(A("d1"), A("Top")), S2(A("d2"), A("Child")), S2(A("query"), A("A verb")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("d1"), A("Domain")), S2(A("d2"), A("Domain"))))))),
"SYNTHESIZE SEPARATES A VIOLATION FROM AN OBLIGATION NOTHING CAN SATISFY (2026-09-10). This store forbids Message uses Dash and obliges every Message to be natural, and m1 breaks the first while nothing on earth could settle the second: MessageIsNatural has no rule, no derivation marking and no rows, so every Message violates it forever. Both arrive as deontic verdicts and they are not the same thing. The dash is the answer, the naturalness is UNCHECKED and waits for llm:validate_judge, and the verdict is F because the decided half is not empty. Counting the second as a failure is what would make a repair loop run to its bound and fail whatever the draft said.",
DEF("case:synthesize-separates-unchecked-from-violated", S2(A("synthesize"), S2(A("m1"), S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S2(S3(A("DEO:m:MessageIsNatural#1"), A("mandatory"), S1(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), N(1)))), S3(A("DEO:p:MessageUsesDash#1"), A("prohibited"), S2(A("MessageUsesDash"), A("MessageUsesDash")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S2(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message"))), S2(A("MessageUsesDash"), S2(A("Message"), A("Dash")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S1(S2(A("m1"), A("Message")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:readings"), S1(S1(S3(A("MessageUsesDash"), S2(A("Message"), A("Dash")), S1(S3(A("{0}"), A("uses"), A("{1}"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S2(S5(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("MessageUsesDash"), S2(A("Message"), A("Dash")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageUsesDash"), S1(S2(A("m1"), A("-")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageIsNatural"), PHI()))))))),
"AND A JUDGEMENT ASSERTED AS A FACT MOVES THE OBLIGATION OUT OF THE UNCHECKED LEDGER, which is how the semantic leg closes without any new mechanism. The judge -- llm:validate_judge, the registered class resolution.md declares -- does not need a door of its own: its verdict IS the fact `Message is natural`, asserted through the same write path as any other. Here m1 has been judged and m2 has not, so the obligation is no longer unjudgeable -- something evidently produces those rows -- and m2's missing judgement is a decided violation. Judge nothing and every message is UNCHECKED and the verdict is T; judge one and the rest are answerable; judge all and there is nothing outstanding. That is the short loop terminating, and the only thing still missing is the caller that asks the judge.",
DEF("case:a-judgement-decides-what-was-unchecked", S2(A("synthesize"), S2(A("m1"), S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:m:MessageIsNatural#1"), A("mandatory"), S1(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:readings"), S1(S1(S3(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")), S1(S3(A("{0}"), A("is"), A("natural"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("m1"), A("Message")), S2(A("m2"), A("Message")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(S1(A("m1")))))))))),
"AND AN OBLIGATION NOTHING VIOLATES IS STILL ON THE LEDGER, which is what both ledgers being built from the VIOLATIONS lost. `It is obligatory that each Message is natural` over an empty Message population produces no violation row, so the old unchecked fold -- which ran over cmd:deo_viols -- never saw the obligation at all, and neither did checked. Twelve of support.auto.dev's eighteen obligations sat in that hole on 2026-09-10, every semantic one among them: truthful, natural, written in the house register, brief. A caller reading `15 decided, 3 unjudgeable` could not tell that truthfulness had never been assessed. The ledger is the OBLIGATIONS now, so an obligation appears in exactly one of decided or unchecked unless something judged it and found no fault, and this case is the one the old shape answered empty.",
DEF("case:an-obligation-nothing-violates-is-still-unchecked", S2(A("synthesize"), S2(A("m1"), S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:m:MessageIsNatural#1"), A("mandatory"), S1(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:readings"), S1(S1(S3(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")), S1(S3(A("{0}"), A("is"), A("natural"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("MessageIsNatural"), S1(A("Message")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageIsNatural"), PHI()))))))),
"AND AN OBLIGATION THE MODEL WROTE BY NAME IS ON THE LEDGER TOO. state:deontics is what the ORACLE finds in NORMA -- deontic set constraints and prohibited populations -- and support.auto.dev's own deontic rules are neither: `de-arbitration-misstated` and its eleven siblings are Constraint instances in the metamodel's population, declared as instance facts, so NORMA has no object for them and the cell cannot carry them. `Constraint awaits a decider` is the model's own word for one with no Predicate behind it (core.md:1110's sibling, Sam's 2026-09-05 split between what a regex or population check decides and what an llm must), it derives and delivers, and an obligation nothing can judge is exactly what this ledger holds. No deontics cell here at all: the store is one population and the ledger still finds it.",
DEF("case:a-constraint-awaiting-a-decider-is-unchecked", S2(A("synthesize"), S2(A("x"), S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("ConstraintAwaitsADecider"), S1(A("Constraint")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("ConstraintAwaitsADecider"), S1(A("Constraint")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("ConstraintAwaitsADecider"), S1(S1(A("de-arbitration-misstated")))))))))),
"AND A NEGATED LEG SUBTRACTS ROWS, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT CLAIM FROM THE SHAPE IT IS WRITTEN IN (2026-09-11). ProbeTests pins the recipe the oracle emits for `Authority is currently in force iff Authority has some Effective Date and that Effective Date is in the past and Authority has no Supersession Date`, and it stood red for a while against a correct emitter: the subtraction had moved inside the projection -- from minus(proj(chain), proj(SupersessionDate)) to proj(minus(chain, joinon(chain, SupersessionDate))) -- and a golden over the text cannot tell a re-association that preserves the answer from one that destroys it. This is the answer. a1 qualifies; a2 has a past effective date and IS superseded; a3's date is not in the past; a4 is superseded with no past date. Only a1 is in force. The hazard the shape invites is a minus whose operands are different widths, which subtracts nothing and answers a1 AND a2 -- measured here at width 3 against width 3, and this case is what would catch it becoming otherwise.",
DEF("case:a-negated-leg-subtracts-the-superseded-rows", S2(A("query"), S2(
    S3(A("proj"),
       S3(A("minus"),
          S5(A("joinon"), A("AuthorityHasEffectiveDate"), A("EffectiveDateIsInThePast"), S1(S2(N(2), N(1))), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3))),
          S5(A("joinon"),
             S5(A("joinon"), A("AuthorityHasEffectiveDate"), A("EffectiveDateIsInThePast"), S1(S2(N(2), N(1))), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3))),
             A("AuthorityHasSupersessionDate"), S1(S2(N(1), N(1))), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
       S1(N(1))),
    S3(S2(A("AuthorityHasEffectiveDate"), S4(S2(A("a1"), A("d1")), S2(A("a2"), A("d2")), S2(A("a3"), A("d3")), S2(A("a4"), A("d4")))),
       S2(A("EffectiveDateIsInThePast"), S2(S1(A("d1")), S1(A("d2")))),
       S2(A("AuthorityHasSupersessionDate"), S2(S2(A("a2"), A("s1")), S2(A("a4"), A("s2")))))))),
DEF("case:form-lookup", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S2(A("CONST"), A("joinon")), A("derive:forms"))),
    PHI())),
"AND A FORM THAT NAMES NOTHING REFUSES RATHER THAN JOINING. This case is one of the deliberate refusals: it is here to fail loudly if the fallback ever comes back.",
DEF("case:unknown-form-refuses", S2(A("derive:delta_eval"),
    S2(S2(A("bogus"), A("F1")), S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
"A TRIGGER FACT MOVES THE ENTITY, AND ONLY FROM THE RIGHT STATUS. main:st_fired is the step the fold applies once per transition: given a transition and a store it answers the entities the event moved. The second case is the guard -- both sales carry SaleIsSubmitted, but o2 is already placed and a submit leaves draft, so o2 must NOT move. Without it the case would pass on an implementation that moved every entity holding the event fact.",
DEF("case:trigger-advances", S2(A("main:st_fired"), S2(S3(A("draft"), A("Sale is submitted"), A("placed")), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("SaleIsSubmitted"), S1(S1(A("o1"))))), S1(S2(A("o1"), A("draft"))))))),
DEF("case:trigger-guarded", S2(A("main:st_fired"), S2(S3(A("draft"), A("Sale is submitted"), A("placed")), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("SaleIsSubmitted"), S2(S1(A("o1")), S1(A("o2"))))), S2(S2(A("o1"), A("draft")), S2(A("o2"), A("placed"))))))),
"COMPILE OUTPUT THAT LANDS WHERE THE TOOL SURFACE READS. The first two pin the compiled shape: a reading names its own fact type and its own players, the capitalized words being the object types. The third is the landing itself -- a two-cell store, one declared fact type, and a reading compiled INTO it, answered through mcp:tools so the assertion is what a caller would actually be offered. Without it the compile could be correct and still write nowhere, which is exactly the state this replaced.",
DEF("case:compile-declares", S2(A("compile:declared_of"), A("Law is scoped to Domain"))),
DEF("case:compile-fts", S2(A("compile:fts_of"), A("Claim is witnessed by Stage"))),
