"AND A POPULATION CAN BE READ BACK. GET answered Theorem 2-s nav leg and no method returned ROWS, so the MCP surface could POST into any of 246 fact types and query none of them. The fillers restrict by PREFIX and both edges fall out of theta:zip rather than a branch: zip truncates to the shorter side, so one filler against a two-column row compares role 1 and ignores role 2, and an EMPTY filler zips to nothing while fp:and_all of nothing is T, so a bare read matches every row. Codd-s theta-restriction with the identity restriction as its zero case. The third one is the case that matters -- a filler nobody holds answers nothing rather than falling back to the whole population, which is the shape a read like this fails in.",
DEF("case:read-all-rows", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), A("main:read_rows")), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S3(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), PHI()))),
DEF("case:read-restricts-by-filler", S2(A("main:read_rows"), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S3(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(A("cal"))))),
DEF("case:read-filler-matches-nothing", S2(A("main:read_rows"), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S3(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(A("zoe"))))),
"AND A FILLER CAN SKIP A ROLE. Prefix matching could restrict roles 1..n and could never ask about role 2 alone -- which agents hold 555-0200 was unaskable, and that is half the questions worth asking of a binary. An EMPTY filler element is the wildcard. The last case is the guard: a wildcard in EVERY position restricts nothing and must answer the whole population, not none of it.",
DEF("case:read-wildcard-skips-role-one", S2(A("main:read_rows"), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S3(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A(""), A("555-0200"))))),
DEF("case:read-wildcard-everywhere-is-all", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), A("main:read_rows")), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S3(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A(""), A(""))))),
"AND THE SUBSET CONSTRAINTS ARE CHECKED. state:setcmp carried nine of them with NO READER -- emitted for a leg-filling mechanism that the corrected matcher replaced -- and this is the reader. It is the tollens direction of the same implication the matcher uses forwards: sub contained in super, so a value in the sub leg absent from the super leg is a violation. The MODALITY decides the tag and therefore the gate: an alethic subset tags subset and refuses the commit, a deontic one tags deontic and warns. Same check, same data, opposite consequence.",
DEF("case:subset-satisfied", S2(A("cmd:sub_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:setcmp"), S1(S1(S4(A("subset"), A("alethic"), S1(S2(A("Sub"), N(1))), S1(S2(A("Sup"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Sub"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("Sup"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c"))))))))),
DEF("case:subset-violation-refuses", S2(A("cmd:sub_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:setcmp"), S1(S1(S4(A("subset"), A("alethic"), S1(S2(A("Sub"), N(1))), S1(S2(A("Sup"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Sub"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("z")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("Sup"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))))))))),
DEF("case:subset-deontic-warns", S2(A("cmd:sub_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:setcmp"), S1(S1(S4(A("subset"), A("deontic"), S1(S2(A("Sub"), N(1))), S1(S2(A("Sup"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Sub"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("z")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("Sup"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))))))))),
"AND A RECIPE FORM LIVES IN TWO EVALUATORS, WHICH IS ONE MORE THAN I UPDATED. pairwith went into derive:forms, the table delta_eval dispatches, and NOT into the derive:eval chain -- and that chain tests seven tags and then FALLS THROUGH TO A JOIN, so a pairwith recipe did not fail to match, it matched the join arm and read selector 4 of a 3-element recipe. The lambda already carried the warning, in derive:cannot_produce: naming the arms that are safe fails closed, naming the arms that are unsafe fails silently, on exactly the forms nobody thought of. The fall-through is the same open tag set seen from the other side. It cost nothing at boot because the World Assumption rules reach pairwith through the delta path, and the 570 cases never evaluated one through the full path -- the second case here does, with the source nested, which is where derive:src hands a sub-recipe back to derive:eval.",
DEF("case:eval-pairwith-full-path", S2(A("derive:eval"), S2(S3(A("pairwith"), A("R"), A("tag")), S1(S2(A("R"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("x")), S2(A("b"), A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:eval-pairwith-nested-source", S2(A("derive:eval"), S2(S3(A("pairwith"), S3(A("proj"), A("R"), S1(N(1))), A("closed")), S1(S2(A("R"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("x")), S2(A("b"), A("y")))))))),
"AND THE FALL-THROUGH IS GONE FROM THIS SIDE TOO. case:unknown-form-refuses pins the table path; this pins the full one, which is the path that HAD the silent join and kept it after the other was fixed. It refuses because find_desc answers nothing and there is no arm to fall into, not because anything tests for a bad tag.",
DEF("case:eval-unknown-form-refuses", S2(A("derive:eval"), S2(S3(A("nosuchform"), A("R"), N(1)), S1(S2(A("R"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))))))),
"THE SECOND AGGREGATE FORM, PINNED AGAINST THE FIRST OVER ONE STORE. F1 carries <g1,2>, <g1,3> and <g2,5>: summing column 2 by column 1 answers <g1,5> and <g2,5>, counting by column 1 answers <g1,2> and <g2,1>, and the two cases share every byte of the store. g1's sum is g2's sum by construction, so a form that folded the wrong group or added the wrong column is not hidden by one row -- the same construction case:agg-rule-sum and case:agg-rule-count use on system:compile_agg_rule, run here through the GRAMMAR instead, which is the half that was missing. The count twin is new coverage too: derive:form_count had no case of its own, so the form table's only aggregate was reached by rules:metamodel's arity rule and nothing else.",
DEF("case:eval-sum-groups", S2(A("derive:eval"), S2(S4(A("sum"), A("F1"), N(1), N(2)), S1(S2(A("F1"), S3(S2(A("g1"), N(2)), S2(A("g1"), N(3)), S2(A("g2"), N(5)))))))),
DEF("case:eval-count-groups", S2(A("derive:eval"), S2(S3(A("count"), A("F1"), N(1)), S1(S2(A("F1"), S3(S2(A("g1"), N(2)), S2(A("g1"), N(3)), S2(A("g2"), N(5)))))))),
"AND A NEW FORM LANDS IN THE TWO WHITELISTS OR IT IS WRONG WHERE NO ANSWER SHOWS IT. derive:reads names the forms whose ONLY source is slot 2, and a sum carries its grouping and its over-column in slots 3 and 4, which are functions rather than populations -- left off that list, a sum rule reports READING a column number as though it named a fact type. derive:minus_rights names the settled-required reads, and an aggregate recomputes from the settled store rather than growing with a delta, so a sum whose source a later rule still produces must be held back: left off that list it appears to require nothing settled, the cycle stratifies, and Corollary 6's refusal silently does not happen. This is law:finiteness's own count fixture with the count made a sum, and it must be refused for the reason the count is.",
DEF("case:reads-sum-one-source", S2(A("derive:reads"), S4(A("sum"), A("F1"), N(1), N(2)))),
DEF("case:strat-sum-cycle-refused", S2(A("derive:stratified"), S2(S2(A("X"), S4(A("sum"), A("Y"), N(1), N(2))), S2(A("Y"), S3(A("proj"), A("X"), S2(N(1), N(2))))))),
"AND THE FORM IN THE MACHINE, NOT ONLY IN THE EVALUATOR. The cases above each ask one def one question; this runs the whole of derive -- layers, run_layer, round, delta_eval -- over a sum whose source is itself DERIVED, which is the arrangement the stratification is for: F1 is a projection of G, H is the sum of F1, and H must be held until F1 has settled or it folds a population that is still arriving and keeps every wrong total it ever emitted, because a fixpoint unions and never retracts. G's three rows project to F1's three and H answers the same <g1,5> and <g2,5> the evaluator case does, by a different road.",
DEF("case:sum-over-a-derived-source", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:pop"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("H")), A("derive"))),
    S2(S2(S2(A("H"), S4(A("sum"), A("F1"), N(1), N(2))), S2(A("F1"), S3(A("proj"), A("G"), S2(N(1), N(2))))),
       S3(S2(A("G"), S3(S3(A("g1"), N(2), A("z")), S3(A("g1"), N(3), A("z")), S3(A("g2"), N(5), A("z")))), S2(A("F1"), PHI()), S2(A("H"), PHI()))))),
"AND THE RULE NO DATA REACHES STILL HAS A WITNESS. Of 62 rules the metamodel store fires 18; the other 44 are quiet because their inputs are empty, which is what an empty derived population is supposed to mean -- but quiet is also what a rule looks like when it is WRONG, and the two are indistinguishable from the store. This is the Harel inherited edge: a composite Status has its sub-machine (Status has sub-machine State Machine Definition, 2026-10-07; until then State Machine Definition was a subtype of Status and the from-Status was the machine itself), so a Transition from the composite induces an effective transition out of every child Status defined in its sub-machine. No transition in the metamodel is from a machine, so the rule contributed nothing and would have rotted silently. Given a store that reaches it, it emits one edge per child and the direct rule emits the machine-s own, which is the union the FORML comment says it over-emits on purpose.",
"AND AN INSTANCE HAS A MACHINE, which nothing measured because nothing had one. `State Machine is for Object Type Instance` and `State Machine is currently in Status` (instances.md) held 0 rows in every store ever built -- support.auto.dev, 4,343 instances and 15 State Machine Definitions, 0 and 0 -- so the store could answer no question about what state anything was in and the rules above them ran on nothing. These three ask the reflection what it answers for the smallest store that has a machine at all: one Object Type with a definition, two instances, one declared initial status and one transition out of it. The machine id is minted on a dot the way reflect:role_one mints a role id; the status is main:status_pop, which is the seed advanced by the fired-transition fold and the same computation `actions` answers a menu from; and the definition is main:noun_machine, which is the fact `State Machine Definition is for Object Type` and not the noun spelled like the machine. With no event asserted the fold moves nothing, so all three read the seed.",
DEF("case:machine-for-each-instance",
    S2(A("reflect:machines"), S7(S3(A("CELL"), A("StateMachineDefinitionIsForObjectType"), S1(S2(A("M"), A("T")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsFromStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("a")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsToStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("b")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsTriggeredByEventType"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("e1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("x1"), A("T")), S2(A("x2"), A("T"))))))),
DEF("case:machine-seeded-at-effective-initial",
    S2(A("reflect:mstatus"), S7(S3(A("CELL"), A("StateMachineDefinitionIsForObjectType"), S1(S2(A("M"), A("T")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsFromStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("a")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsToStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("b")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsTriggeredByEventType"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("e1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("x1"), A("T")), S2(A("x2"), A("T"))))))),
DEF("case:machine-is-instance-of-its-definition",
    S2(A("reflect:msmd"), S7(S3(A("CELL"), A("StateMachineDefinitionIsForObjectType"), S1(S2(A("M"), A("T")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsFromStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("a")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsToStatus"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("b")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsTriggeredByEventType"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("e1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TransitionIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("t1"), A("M")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), S2(S2(A("x1"), A("T")), S2(A("x2"), A("T"))))))),
"AND A NESTED MACHINE IS STILL DEFINED IN THE ONE OUTSIDE IT. A composite Status has its sub-machine (Status has sub-machine State Machine Definition, 2026-10-07; until then a State Machine Definition was a Status), so an app groups states by declaring one, and the three flat rules for `Status is defined in State Machine Definition` stopped at the innermost: support.auto.dev declares Draft in Open and Open in Support Request, and Draft was in Support Request nowhere. Nothing noticed while no machine instance existed; the moment one did, core.mds subset constraint (a machines status must be defined in the definition it is an instance of) refused every write to the app. The containment is transitive and the rule now says so. Two declared rows of the nesting and the sub-machine of Open, three after the closure.",
DEF("case:status-defined-through-a-nested-machine",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:pop"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition")), S3(A("COMP"), A("derive"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), A("rules:metamodel")), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), K(S1(S2(S2(A("StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition"), S2(S2(A("Draft"), A("Open")), S2(A("Open"), A("SR")))), S2(A("StatusHasSubMachineStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("Open"), A("Open")))))))))))), PHI())),
DEF("case:harel-inherited-edge", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:pop"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("StatusHasEffectiveTransitionToStatusOnEventType")), S3(A("COMP"), A("derive"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), A("rules:metamodel")), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), K(S1(S5(S2(A("TransitionIsFromStatus"), S1(S2(A("t9"), A("M")))), S2(A("TransitionIsToStatus"), S1(S2(A("t9"), A("done")))), S2(A("TransitionIsTriggeredByEventType"), S1(S2(A("t9"), A("e9")))), S2(A("StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("M")), S2(A("b"), A("M")))), S2(A("StatusHasSubMachineStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("M"), A("M")))))))))))), PHI())),
"AND A COLUMN TYPE IS LOOKED UP, NOT ASSUMED. rmap:ddl_colspec hardcoded TEXT and the two DDL cases call rmap:ddl_table directly with a hand-built spec, so the colspec path had NO coverage -- which is why 313 columns could be TEXT while the suite stayed green. These pin the lookup itself: a declared value type resolves through ObjectTypeHasConceptualDataType to the catalogue and out to its abstract SQL type, and an undeclared one falls back to TEXT rather than bottoming. The fallback case is the one that matters, because 208 of the 313 columns are NORMA reference-mode id mints that declare nothing.",
DEF("case:sqltype-declared-decimal", S2(A("rmap:sqltype"), S2(A("Amount"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeHasConceptualDataType"), S2(S2(A("Amount"), A("decimal")), S2(A("Arity"), A("integer")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ConceptualDataTypeHasAbstractSQLType"), S2(S2(A("decimal"), A("DECIMAL")), S2(A("integer"), A("INTEGER")))))))),
DEF("case:sqltype-declared-integer", S2(A("rmap:sqltype"), S2(A("Arity"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeHasConceptualDataType"), S2(S2(A("Amount"), A("decimal")), S2(A("Arity"), A("integer")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ConceptualDataTypeHasAbstractSQLType"), S2(S2(A("decimal"), A("DECIMAL")), S2(A("integer"), A("INTEGER")))))))),
DEF("case:sqltype-undeclared-defaults-text", S2(A("rmap:sqltype"), S2(A("Nope"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeHasConceptualDataType"), S2(S2(A("Amount"), A("decimal")), S2(A("Arity"), A("integer")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("ConceptualDataTypeHasAbstractSQLType"), S2(S2(A("decimal"), A("DECIMAL")), S2(A("integer"), A("INTEGER")))))))),
"AND A JOIN-PATH LEG IS ACTUALLY JOINED. Eight of the nine set-comparison constraints in the metamodel have legs of two members and every one of them has at least one EMPTY source there -- EventIsOfEventType 0, FactIsOfFactType 0, RoleIsUsedInReading 0 -- so the checker answered 0 whether it worked or not, which is precisely how it went eight years unexercised. These two reach it. Each leg is <A.1, B.1> with a path <A,2,1> <B,2,1>: join A and B on column 2, project column 1 of each, and the second output column is offset by the arity of the first because joinon numbers its output over the concatenation. Sub joins to <a1,b1>; sup joins to <a1,b1> and the constraint holds, or to <a1,b9> and it does not.",
DEF("case:subset-joinpath-holds", S2(A("cmd:sub_viols"), S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S4(S5(A("A"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("B"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("C"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("D"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:setcmp"), S1(S1(S5(A("subset"), A("alethic"), S2(S2(A("A"), N(1)), S2(A("B"), N(1))), S2(S2(A("C"), N(1)), S2(A("D"), N(1))), S2(S2(S3(A("A"), N(2), N(1)), S3(A("B"), N(2), N(1))), S2(S3(A("C"), N(2), N(1)), S3(A("D"), N(2), N(1)))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("A"), S1(S2(A("a1"), A("k1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("B"), S1(S2(A("b1"), A("k1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("C"), S1(S2(A("a1"), A("k2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("D"), S1(S2(A("b1"), A("k2"))))))),
DEF("case:subset-joinpath-violated", S2(A("cmd:sub_viols"), S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S4(S5(A("A"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("B"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("C"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())), S5(A("D"), S2(A("P"), A("K")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:setcmp"), S1(S1(S5(A("subset"), A("alethic"), S2(S2(A("A"), N(1)), S2(A("B"), N(1))), S2(S2(A("C"), N(1)), S2(A("D"), N(1))), S2(S2(S3(A("A"), N(2), N(1)), S3(A("B"), N(2), N(1))), S2(S3(A("C"), N(2), N(1)), S3(A("D"), N(2), N(1)))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("A"), S1(S2(A("a1"), A("k1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("B"), S1(S2(A("b1"), A("k1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("C"), S1(S2(A("a1"), A("k2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("D"), S1(S2(A("b9"), A("k2"))))))),
"AND A NESTED POPULATION IS ONE ROW PER FACT, NOT ONE ROW PER GROUP. A carrier may store a population grouped by its first column, and ast:pop_sub answered the whole group as a SINGLE element -- so distl made one pair and apndl concatenated it, and CaseObservesObservation, declared arity 2, came back as three rows of widths 6, 5 and 5. A binary fact type cannot have a six-column row. Every reader of system:pop_rows saw that shape: the mandatory check, the matcher, main:read_rows, and law:closed, which compares the accessor against the fixpoint with seteq and could never make three wide rows equal thirteen binary ones -- which is the whole of store-closed. The group key distributes over the values: an atom becomes a one-column row and a row stays a row, so a grouped ternary still prepends its key.",
DEF("case:pop-nested-binary", S2(A("system:pop_rows"), S2(A("R"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("R"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("a"), S2(A("x"), A("z"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("b"), S1(A("y"))))))))))),
DEF("case:pop-nested-keeps-rows", S2(A("system:pop_rows"), S2(A("R"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("R"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("c"), S1(S2(A("p"), A("q")))))))))))),

"EVERY DERIVATION MARKER IS A CLAIM THAT A RULE EXISTS, and law:marker_marked read only two of the four modes, so a `+` or a subtype marking with no deliverer was outside the law and answered nothing. These pin all four: the mixed store answers every name, and the two single-mode stores answer the one name each -- both of which were EMPTY before 2026-09-05, which is the whole shape of the miss. The law reads the marker surface, so a case over a literal store is the unit of the change; the corpus records and the base report cover it composed.",
DEF("case:marker-marked-spans-modes", S2(A("law:marker_marked"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:derived"), S1(S4(S2(A("Alpha"), A("full")), S2(A("Beta"), A("semi")), S2(A("Gamma"), A("subtype")), S2(A("Delta"), A("stored")))))))),
DEF("case:marker-marked-semi-alone", S2(A("law:marker_marked"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:derived"), S1(S1(S2(A("Beta"), A("semi")))))))),
DEF("case:marker-marked-subtype-alone", S2(A("law:marker_marked"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:derived"), S1(S1(S2(A("Gamma"), A("subtype")))))))),

"A COLLAPSE YIELDS ITS CHILDREN IN THE PREFERRED IDENTIFIER'S ROLE ORDER (C.2.c). The mapping inputs arrive sorted by name, so `Measurement Window(.Start, .End)` collapsed into `Customer calls API in Measurement Window with Call Volume` as End, Start, and support.auto.dev's uniqueness and primary key disagreed with NORMA's on that one table (2026-09-09). cn:pidsort orders a collapse's children by their identifying fact's position in the preferred uniqueness that holds it, the row state:ucs carries in declaration order; a child whose fact no preferred uniqueness holds keeps the order it came in, after the held ones. Two stores: the scheme facts arriving reversed, and one unheld child among them.",
